# Define a public-safe reconstruction algorithm gate with bounded similarity

Treat the maintainer's complete lawful installation as the internal 100%
reference for coverage analysis, but do not generate the distributable algorithm
directly from that complete installation and do not publish an artifact that
embeds or reconstructs it without the user's lawful local source. Treat roughly
60% as the current planning estimate for a possible minimum installation and
45–55% as the tentative candidate-similarity window; validate both concepts
empirically before code uses them.

## Acceptance criteria

- [x] Specify what “100% installation”, “minimum installation”, and “similarity”
  mean in measurable, content-safe terms before choosing a production metric;
  treat the current ~60% minimum-installation estimate as planning only.
- [ ] Do not author a distributable `algorithm/*.txt` plan by serializing,
  full-tree diffing, or otherwise turning the complete 100% reference
  installation into direct recipe material.
- [ ] Keep the original user installation read-only throughout validation and
  reconstruction; algorithm execution writes only to SHAR-owned output/cache.
- [ ] Evaluate the tentative 45–55% similarity window; do not hardcode it until
  false-positive and false-negative evidence exists across lawful installs.
- [ ] Ensure passing similarity can never replace required local source bytes,
  exact-file gates, hashes, structural validation, or cryptographic/provenance
  checks.
- [ ] Ensure distributable family plans under `algorithm/*.txt` use the
  implemented `shar.algorithm.v1` source-bound contract and never become a
  reversible encoding of the maintainer's private complete installation.
- [ ] Keep plan-contained hashes limited to exact source binding and protected
  target verification. They are not an antivirus verdict and must never replace
  caller-supplied source bytes.
- [ ] Document the finalized metric and limitations in README when implemented;
  do not describe the tentative percentage as a legal safe harbor.

## Dependencies

Depends on representative source-installation evidence and final product
admission integration. The generic `shar.algorithm.v1` serialization mechanism
already exists and is not itself a similarity threshold.

## Evidence

`docs/technical/security/public-safe-reconstruction-gate.md` now defines the
100% reference, minimum installation, reference coverage, and a candidate
weighted-Jaccard similarity metric in content-safe count-vector terms. The
private reference contributes aggregate obfuscated directory/extension counts,
not payload bytes, file names, or a reversible full-tree recipe.

`tools/source-similarity/main.py` implements only evidence measurement using
exact rational arithmetic. Four stdlib tests cover complete, missing, extra,
and invalid vectors and prove the evidence object has no admission result. No
45–55% threshold exists in that code. The technical contract also pins the
read-only source boundary, source-bound plan publication restrictions, and the
rule that similarity cannot substitute for any hard source gate.

The ~60% minimum-installation estimate and 45–55% similarity window remain
design targets, not validated production evidence, legal conclusions, or safe
harbors. The task remains open for representative-install calibration and final
algorithm serialization/admission integration.
